1
00:00:02,030 --> 00:00:04,990
Hello English learners, welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:05,770
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:06,710
And my name is Erica.

4
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:12,890
Today we're bringing you another one of our sports lessons and this time we're going to go to a boxing match.

5
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:14,520
That's right.

6
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:19,950
So we're going to be learning a language that you can use if you're talking about boxing, but not only boxing, right?

7
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:21,280
Right.

8
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:24,710
We can also use this language for describing a fight or somebody that's fighting.

9
00:00:25,430 --> 00:00:32,630
Okay, so not that we're asking our listeners to get into fights, but if ever you see a fight, at least now you'll have the language to talk about it.

10
00:00:32,850 --> 00:00:33,310
Exactly.

11
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:37,920
So let's listen to a dialogue for the first time.

12
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:38,630
It might sound familiar.

13
00:00:38,930 --> 00:00:45,310
We have the legendary Rocky Balboa at EnglishPod fighting for the heavyweight championship.

14
00:00:45,830 --> 00:00:46,810
So let's see what happens.

15
00:00:51,590 --> 00:00:53,090
Welcome back, boxing fans.

16
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:58,600
My name is Rick Fields and here with me is the man with the iron jaw, Bob Copeland.

17
00:00:58,600 --> 00:00:58,850
Thank you, Rick.

18
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:01,010
We're coming to you live from Las Vegas.

19
00:01:01,860 --> 00:01:09,350
We're in the beautiful MGM Grand Hotel and Casino where the World Heavyweight Championship is about to get underway.

20
00:01:10,420 --> 00:01:10,910
That's right, Bob.

21
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:20,090
We're about to witness the legendary Italian stallion himself, Rocky Balboa, square off against his lifetime rival, Apollo Creed.

22
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:22,650
This will be a gruesome match for sure.

23
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:26,770
Both fighters are in the ring and we are about to begin.

24
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:46,740
In the blue corner, weighing in at 220 pounds, the former heavyweight champion of the world, the master of disaster, the one and only Apollo Creed.

25
00:01:46,740 --> 00:02:04,750
In the red corner, weighing 218 pounds and with a record of 47 wins and 37 knockouts, the undefeated, undisputed heavyweight champion of the world, the Italian stallion, Rocky Balboa.

26
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:11,930
There's the bell and this fight is underway.

27
00:02:12,850 --> 00:02:14,930
Apollo quickly attacks Rocky with quick, strong jabs.

28
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:19,940
He dodges successfully and counterattacks with a strong right hook.

29
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:20,350
Apollo is cut.

30
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:24,930
Rocky landed a strong blow to his right eyebrow and cut him.

31
00:02:26,180 --> 00:02:26,860
This is his chance.

32
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:30,680
Rocky quickly throws a left, right, another left.

33
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:34,060
Apollo is getting pounded.

34
00:02:34,060 --> 00:02:38,740
Apollo recovers with a powerful haymaker and catches Rocky off guard.

35
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:38,960
He's down.

36
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:42,740
The ref starts the count.

37
00:02:42,740 --> 00:02:55,740
One, two, three, four, five.

38
00:02:55,740 --> 00:02:56,790
Wow sounds like a great fight.

39
00:02:57,900 --> 00:02:59,560
Amazing fight and I don't know who's going to win.

40
00:03:00,020 --> 00:03:01,260
Rocky apparently got knocked down.

41
00:03:01,450 --> 00:03:02,150
What do you think will happen?

42
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:06,860
Well, if I know anything about Rocky Balboa, he will not stay knocked down for long.

43
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:07,330
All right.

44
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:12,110
Let's take a look at some of the language we saw in this dialogue in Language Takeaway.

45
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:15,140
Language Takeaway.

46
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:21,220
Well, Rick Fields said that we have a very gruesome match tonight.

47
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:23,420
A gruesome match.

48
00:03:23,860 --> 00:03:24,420
Gruesome.

49
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:25,740
All right.

50
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:26,760
So, if something is gruesome.

51
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:30,460
It's really, it's bad, it's terrible, it's bloody.

52
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:32,100
Okay.

53
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:36,720
Bloody, terrible, very hard or like a gruesome fight would be a very bloody fight.

54
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:38,280
Yeah, or a gruesome film.

55
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:39,300
Okay.

56
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:40,280
Very good.

57
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:41,280
So, gruesome.

58
00:03:41,940 --> 00:03:45,300
Now, they mentioned that both fighters are in the ring.

59
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:48,050
Both fighters are in the ring.

60
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:49,380
The ring.

61
00:03:49,390 --> 00:03:49,750
Okay.

62
00:03:49,750 --> 00:03:52,750
So, the ring is the physical space where they fight.

63
00:03:53,190 --> 00:03:53,890
But isn't it square?

64
00:03:54,330 --> 00:03:55,570
Yeah, it is square.

65
00:03:55,820 --> 00:03:58,550
It is not really circular, but it's called a boxing ring.

66
00:03:58,770 --> 00:03:59,730
I'm not really sure why.

67
00:04:00,030 --> 00:04:05,290
Well, maybe one of our listeners knows and can tell us about that because I am not an expert on boxing.

68
00:04:05,790 --> 00:04:08,670
But what I do know is our next word.

69
00:04:08,990 --> 00:04:15,650
They were describing Rocky Balboa's career and they said he had 37 knockouts.

70
00:04:16,210 --> 00:04:16,810
Okay.

71
00:04:17,090 --> 00:04:17,630
A knockout.

72
00:04:18,210 --> 00:04:18,590
A knockout.

73
00:04:19,250 --> 00:04:21,490
In this sense, knockout is a noun.

74
00:04:21,990 --> 00:04:32,390
A knockout is when the fighter hits the other fighter and he falls to the ground and he can't get up in 10 seconds because he's tired, he's dizzy, he's hurt.

75
00:04:32,620 --> 00:04:33,620
Okay.

76
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:34,090
So, that's a knockout.

77
00:04:34,620 --> 00:04:35,620
Okay.

78
00:04:35,620 --> 00:04:38,080
So, it's a technical boxing word.

79
00:04:39,100 --> 00:04:41,880
It's the action of falling to the ground and not getting up.

80
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:43,380
Right.

81
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:46,580
But there's also a verb, to knock out.

82
00:04:46,580 --> 00:04:47,820
Or to knock someone out.

83
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:48,520
Exactly.

84
00:04:49,380 --> 00:04:51,300
So, when you say, I knocked this guy out.

85
00:04:52,180 --> 00:04:57,840
It means I hit him so hard that he fell to the floor and maybe he was unconscious for a while.

86
00:04:58,460 --> 00:04:59,160
So, he fell asleep basically.

87
00:04:59,460 --> 00:04:59,620
Almost.

88
00:05:00,460 --> 00:05:01,460
Yeah.

89
00:05:01,460 --> 00:05:02,460
So, it's very similar.

90
00:05:02,460 --> 00:05:03,460
The noun form to the verb form.

91
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:06,300
So, basically, if you knock someone out, you get a knockout.

92
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:06,460
Okay.

93
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:09,260
So, to knock someone out, a knockout.

94
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:09,740
Alright.

95
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:17,660
Now, for our next word, we saw as Apollo quickly began to jab Rocky Balboa.

96
00:05:18,300 --> 00:05:19,540
So, the noun, a jab.

97
00:05:20,140 --> 00:05:20,960
So, a jab.

98
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:22,820
Quick jabs.

99
00:05:22,860 --> 00:05:25,060
So, a jab is a kind of punch, right?

100
00:05:25,340 --> 00:05:27,740
It's a fast, short, but not very strong punch.

101
00:05:28,140 --> 00:05:28,400
Okay.

102
00:05:28,460 --> 00:05:31,170
So, a technical boxing word, a jab.

103
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:32,460
A jab.

104
00:05:32,460 --> 00:05:33,820
So, it can also be a verb, right?

105
00:05:33,820 --> 00:05:34,820
Right.

106
00:05:34,820 --> 00:05:35,820
So, to jab.

107
00:05:35,820 --> 00:05:36,010
To jab.

108
00:05:36,820 --> 00:05:40,940
Now, Apollo was throwing jabs and Rocky began to dodge.

109
00:05:40,940 --> 00:05:41,940
Alright.

110
00:05:41,940 --> 00:05:43,610
And that's our next word, to dodge.

111
00:05:44,290 --> 00:05:44,510
Dodge.

112
00:05:45,100 --> 00:05:46,100
D-O-D-G-E.

113
00:05:46,370 --> 00:05:46,770
To dodge.

114
00:05:47,100 --> 00:05:48,100
Alright.

115
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:51,730
So, if you dodge, you're trying to avoid getting hit.

116
00:05:51,990 --> 00:05:52,110
Right.

117
00:05:52,860 --> 00:05:54,450
So, you move your body so that you're not hit.

118
00:05:55,060 --> 00:05:56,060
Exactly.

119
00:05:56,060 --> 00:05:58,180
Now, we can also use it in different ways.

120
00:05:58,180 --> 00:06:00,830
Not only do you dodge when somebody's trying to punch you.

121
00:06:01,420 --> 00:06:03,110
You can also, for example, dodge a bullet.

122
00:06:03,430 --> 00:06:03,650
Right.

123
00:06:03,650 --> 00:06:08,490
So, when you dodge a bullet, you avoid some bad situation or some bad event.

124
00:06:09,050 --> 00:06:09,350
Right.

125
00:06:09,370 --> 00:06:12,670
So, we have many examples of dodge used in other contexts.

126
00:06:12,810 --> 00:06:15,090
So, why don't we listen to some examples?

127
00:06:17,020 --> 00:06:17,310
Example one.

128
00:06:18,310 --> 00:06:19,970
See that guy dodging through the traffic?

129
00:06:20,570 --> 00:06:22,210
He's going to get hit by a car if he's not careful.

130
00:06:23,540 --> 00:06:24,320
Example two.

131
00:06:25,370 --> 00:06:26,930
Well, the storm turned south.

132
00:06:27,140 --> 00:06:29,050
So, it looks like we managed to dodge a bullet.

133
00:06:30,740 --> 00:06:31,120
Example three.

134
00:06:32,030 --> 00:06:34,950
Look, you're the one who is in charge of sales and the numbers are down.

135
00:06:35,510 --> 00:06:37,490
It's your problem, so stop trying to dodge your responsibilities.

136
00:06:38,730 --> 00:06:41,660
You know, that word reminds me of another sport, dodgeball.

137
00:06:41,930 --> 00:06:42,610
Oh, dodgeball.

138
00:06:42,660 --> 00:06:43,010
Exactly.

139
00:06:43,660 --> 00:06:44,660
Same principle.

140
00:06:44,660 --> 00:06:46,590
Yeah, but that's an entirely different lesson.

141
00:06:47,380 --> 00:06:50,650
I think we need to focus here on the boxing vocabulary.

142
00:06:51,450 --> 00:06:55,630
So, the announcers said that Apollo is getting pounded.

143
00:06:56,490 --> 00:06:57,890
To get pounded.

144
00:06:58,390 --> 00:06:59,050
To get pounded.

145
00:06:59,730 --> 00:07:01,690
Now, if someone is getting pounded.

146
00:07:02,270 --> 00:07:03,850
They're getting hit really hard.

147
00:07:04,310 --> 00:07:05,010
And a lot.

148
00:07:05,580 --> 00:07:06,580
Mm-hmm.

149
00:07:06,580 --> 00:07:07,980
So, to get pounded.

150
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:09,820
Get pounded.

151
00:07:09,820 --> 00:07:15,840
And now, we have one more word and it's also another boxing term called a haymaker.

152
00:07:16,140 --> 00:07:17,320
A powerful haymaker.

153
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:18,060
Okay.

154
00:07:18,260 --> 00:07:21,080
So, Apollo received a powerful haymaker.

155
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:23,180
Now, this word is actually new to me.

156
00:07:23,220 --> 00:07:24,160
So, Marco, what does it mean?

157
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:28,420
Basically, it's a very strong, very wild punch.

158
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:28,840
Okay.

159
00:07:28,920 --> 00:07:31,180
So, you don't have really good form.

160
00:07:31,400 --> 00:07:33,590
Your body is kind of moving all over the place.

161
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:34,030
Right.

162
00:07:34,780 --> 00:07:35,780
So, you don't have much balance.

163
00:07:35,780 --> 00:07:40,770
So, you just throw your entire body and your fist at someone to hit them as hard as you can.

164
00:07:40,950 --> 00:07:41,670
That's a haymaker.

165
00:07:42,540 --> 00:07:43,540
All right.

166
00:07:43,540 --> 00:07:45,060
A technical boxing word here for you guys.

167
00:07:45,060 --> 00:07:48,470
But now, we've got some interesting phrases for you on Fluency Builder.

168
00:07:50,620 --> 00:07:50,900
Fluency Builder.

169
00:07:51,800 --> 00:07:52,380
All right.

170
00:07:52,380 --> 00:07:58,280
So, on Fluency Builder today, we're going to take a look at some of these phrases and we have four of them for you today.

171
00:07:58,420 --> 00:08:00,500
So, let's get started with the first one.

172
00:08:01,260 --> 00:08:07,440
Well, the announcer said that the boxing match was about to get underway.

173
00:08:07,700 --> 00:08:08,780
Okay.

174
00:08:08,780 --> 00:08:09,780
To get underway.

175
00:08:09,780 --> 00:08:10,780
To get underway.

176
00:08:11,140 --> 00:08:13,860
So, again, this is another way of saying about to begin.

177
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:15,100
Right.

178
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:16,100
All right.

179
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:18,250
So, we're about to get underway with this new project.

180
00:08:18,860 --> 00:08:20,310
Or the meeting's just about to get underway.

181
00:08:20,770 --> 00:08:21,750
So, make sure everyone's here.

182
00:08:22,130 --> 00:08:22,650
Very good.

183
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:31,460
So, the match was about to get underway and the sports announcer said that Rocky Balboa is going to square off against his lifetime rival.

184
00:08:31,930 --> 00:08:35,670
So, we're going to look at this phrase, to square off against someone.

185
00:08:35,860 --> 00:08:36,860
All right.

186
00:08:36,860 --> 00:08:39,090
I'm going to square off against Jim.

187
00:08:39,380 --> 00:08:40,700
All right.

188
00:08:40,700 --> 00:08:46,370
When you square off against Jim, you get ready to fight with him or to compete with him, right?

189
00:08:47,380 --> 00:08:48,380
Right.

190
00:08:48,380 --> 00:08:50,260
So, we're about to have a one-to-one competition.

191
00:08:50,610 --> 00:08:55,970
So, this is like a sports word, but it's also really commonly used in a lot of different contexts.

192
00:08:56,460 --> 00:09:01,900
So, why don't we hear a few more examples of how we can use this phrase to square off.

193
00:09:01,900 --> 00:09:02,900
Example one.

194
00:09:03,160 --> 00:09:07,980
The two countries are moving their armies close to the borders as they prepare to square off in a border dispute.

195
00:09:09,900 --> 00:09:10,900
Example two.

196
00:09:11,420 --> 00:09:14,380
Italy and Brazil are squaring off in the final game this afternoon.

197
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:18,340
Example three.

198
00:09:18,340 --> 00:09:21,230
Lisa and Daniel squared off against each other in the marketing meeting today.

199
00:09:21,900 --> 00:09:23,010
They were both pretty angry.

200
00:09:23,780 --> 00:09:25,180
All right.

201
00:09:25,180 --> 00:09:29,490
So, great examples, and I think we can use it now not only in boxing, but in other situations.

202
00:09:30,130 --> 00:09:34,710
Now, Bob said that Rocky landed a strong blow.

203
00:09:35,830 --> 00:09:37,450
To land a strong blow.

204
00:09:37,620 --> 00:09:38,620
All right.

205
00:09:38,620 --> 00:09:40,010
So, land a strong blow.

206
00:09:40,390 --> 00:09:41,690
Now, let's break this down.

207
00:09:41,970 --> 00:09:42,510
So, a blow.

208
00:09:43,130 --> 00:09:44,570
A blow is a punch or a hit.

209
00:09:44,910 --> 00:09:45,110
Okay.

210
00:09:45,170 --> 00:09:47,510
And when you land a strong blow, what happens?

211
00:09:47,970 --> 00:09:52,420
You basically successfully hit your opponent with a punch or hit.

212
00:09:52,720 --> 00:09:55,800
So, you hit them, so your fist meets their body.

213
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:56,180
Yes.

214
00:09:56,260 --> 00:09:58,800
So, your fist landed on their body.

215
00:09:59,020 --> 00:10:00,020
Mm-hmm.

216
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:01,120
So, I could also change this a little bit.

217
00:10:01,240 --> 00:10:03,340
I could say, I landed a punch.

218
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:04,200
Right.

219
00:10:04,900 --> 00:10:08,380
Or in boxing terms, he landed a strong left jab.

220
00:10:08,620 --> 00:10:08,840
Okay.

221
00:10:08,940 --> 00:10:10,480
So, to land a blow.

222
00:10:11,380 --> 00:10:13,760
The announcer described it as Rock threw a left.

223
00:10:14,100 --> 00:10:14,820
To throw a left.

224
00:10:15,340 --> 00:10:17,520
So, he said he threw a left and a right.

225
00:10:18,040 --> 00:10:19,500
Now again, we'll take this one apart.

226
00:10:19,900 --> 00:10:21,020
So, a left.

227
00:10:21,020 --> 00:10:21,700
What are we talking about?

228
00:10:22,020 --> 00:10:25,540
Basically, a left punch, but we can omit punch.

229
00:10:25,940 --> 00:10:26,120
Okay.

230
00:10:26,300 --> 00:10:32,460
So, it's a punch with your left hand, but the verb throw, you throw a left punch.

231
00:10:32,960 --> 00:10:36,040
So, basically, you punch somebody with your left.

232
00:10:36,540 --> 00:10:36,740
Okay.

233
00:10:36,840 --> 00:10:39,300
So, it's just another way of saying to punch someone.

234
00:10:39,480 --> 00:10:39,980
To punch someone.

235
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:41,500
Right.

236
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:43,940
So, Bob started to throw some punches at the other guy.

237
00:10:43,940 --> 00:10:44,940
All right.

238
00:10:44,940 --> 00:10:45,440
Look over there.

239
00:10:45,940 --> 00:10:46,620
That guy's throwing punches.

240
00:10:46,940 --> 00:10:47,940
All right.

241
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:50,200
So, to throw a left.

242
00:10:50,460 --> 00:10:51,340
To throw a punch.

243
00:10:51,820 --> 00:10:55,900
Now, again, you can use this in a lot of different ways with a lot of different types of punches.

244
00:10:56,260 --> 00:10:57,520
So, you could say to throw a jab.

245
00:10:58,040 --> 00:10:58,380
Okay.

246
00:10:58,460 --> 00:10:59,420
Or throw an uppercut.

247
00:10:59,660 --> 00:11:00,240
Or throw a hook.

248
00:11:00,680 --> 00:11:01,020
Okay.

249
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:09,320
So, now that we've seen all these phrases, why don't we listen to the dialogue for the very last time and we'll come back in a little bit.

250
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:14,880
Welcome back, boxing fans.

251
00:11:15,260 --> 00:11:19,220
My name is Rick Fields, and here with me is the man with the iron jaw, Bob Copeland.

252
00:11:19,900 --> 00:11:20,680
Thank you, Rick.

253
00:11:21,180 --> 00:11:22,800
We're coming to you live from Las Vegas.

254
00:11:23,540 --> 00:11:31,180
We're in the beautiful MGM Grand Hotel and Casino, where the World Heavyweight Championship is about to get underway.

255
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:32,740
That's right, Bob.

256
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:41,920
We're about to witness the legendary Italian Stallion himself, Rocky Balboa, square off against his lifetime rival, Apollo Creed.

257
00:11:42,490 --> 00:11:44,470
This will be a gruesome match for sure.

258
00:11:45,390 --> 00:11:48,570
Both fighters are in the ring, and we are about to begin.

259
